; Tutorial.inix -- the first walk through FileDir and the Player.
;
; WHAT THIS FILE IS
;
; One step to a section, in the order a person would do them. Each step says
; three things: what a narrator should explain, which key to press, and what a
; screen reader says in answer.
;
;   Say=    the narration, in plain words
;   Key=    the key to press, written the way the documents write keys
;   Hear=   one line of what JAWS says, repeated for each line it says
;   Note=   a remark for the reader of the written version; not spoken
;
; The Hear lines are the point of the format. They come from real speech
; histories rather than from anybody's idea of what ought to be said, so the
; written tutorial matches what a person actually hears, and the same file can
; be replayed later to check that it still does.
;
; The settings assumed are the factory ones, with tutor messages limited to
; controls that are not standard, and punctuation not announced.
;
; The two searches use a real directory: the Access On show, whose episodes
; mention the BrailleNote Evolve in a title and the Ray Ban Meta Glasses only in
; a summary. That contrast is the whole reason both searches exist.
;
; makeTutorial.py turns this into a section of Tutorials.md.
; sayTutorial.cmd turns it into a spoken walk, clearly announced as a
; simulation.

[about]
Title=A First Walk Through FileDir
Intro=This is a simulation, not a recording.
Setup=FileDir is open on a folder of saved podcast directories, and JAWS is running.

[step]
Say=Every folder opens as a list you arrow through. JAWS says the folder and how many items are in it.
Key=
Hear=FileDir
Hear=List box C:\PodcastDirectories
Hear=119 items
Note=The count comes once, when the folder opens.

[step]
Say=Control plus J jumps to a file by name. FileDir asks for the text and remembers your last ten answers.
Key=Control+J
Hear=Jump
Hear=Text edit combo
Note=The window is named Jump because the key is J.

[step]
Say=Type access and press Enter.
Key=Enter
Hear=AccessOn.htm
Note=Jump looks at names. Control plus K looks inside the files.

[step]
Say=This page lists the episodes of a podcast. Control plus Shift plus L plays what is in it: FileDir reads the page, finds the media links, and opens the Player.
Key=Control+Shift+L
Hear=Play list
Hear=Looking for media links in AccessOn.htm
Hear=AccessOn.htm
Hear=Track list, 92 tracks from links in AccessOn.htm: List box
Note=The window is named after the play list until something plays. Nothing plays yet.

[step]
Say=The Player is a dialog, so Tab moves through it. In order: the track list, extra info, the sort order, next and previous track, forward and backward, the jump size, the two chapter buttons, where in track, rate, volume, then execute, stop, defaults, help and close.
Key=Tab
Hear=Extra Info: edit read only
Note=Every control has its own Alt key: T for the track list, X for extra info, O for order, E for execute, S for stop, H for help.

[step]
Say=Three keys do most of the work. Enter plays the track the cursor is on. Space plays and pauses from anywhere but a button. Escape closes the Player.
Key=Shift+Tab
Hear=Track list, 92 tracks from links in AccessOn.htm: List box
Hear=Access On is coming soon
Note=Shift plus Tab goes back the way you came.

[step]
Say=The track list holds one line for each episode: its name, and its length where the page gave one. There is no number in front, so pressing a letter jumps to the first episode beginning with it.
Key=Down Arrow
Hear=Welcome to Access on, and AI to help you use your technology, 1 colon 09 colon 23
Note=Arrowing through the list chooses nothing and plays nothing.

[step]
Say=Press Enter to play the one you are on.
Key=Enter
Hear=Playing
Note=Now the window title is the track, so your screen reader's title key says what is playing.

[step]
Say=There are two modes, and Enter follows them. While something plays, Enter is stop. While nothing plays, Enter is execute playback. The status line says which: press your screen reader's key for it.
Key=
Hear=Playing track 2 of 92, 12 min 3 sec of 1 hr 9 min
Note=The status line is never announced. It waits until you ask.

[step]
Say=Control with an arrow is the transport. Sideways is time inside the track, up and down is tracks in the queue, the Page keys are chapters, and adding Shift means all the way.
Key=Control+Right Arrow
Hear=13 min 3 sec of 1 hr 9 min
Note=The jump is a minute to begin with. Alt plus I offers fifteen seconds up to an hour.

[step]
Say=Press Space to pause.
Key=Space
Hear=Paused
Note=Space plays and pauses from any control except a button, where Space presses the button.

[step]
Say=Now the two searches, which answer different questions. Control plus J jumps by the line the list shows. Type braillenote and press Enter.
Key=Control+J
Hear=Jump
Hear=Text edit combo
Hear=The BrailleNote Evolve from HumanWare
Note=That title has the word in it, so Jump finds it.

[step]
Say=Control plus K searches everything known about each episode: the presenter, the date, the address, and the summary the page gave. Type meta glasses and press Enter.
Key=Control+K
Hear=Keywords
Hear=Text edit combo
Hear=Workaround for the Braille Screen Input Uber bug, Android becomes more restrictive, and assigning a Braille keyboard command to Sound Curtin
Note=Those words are nowhere in that title. They are in the summary, which is why Keywords found it and Jump could not.

[step]
Say=Keywords takes more than one word. An ampersand means both, a bar means either, and a star stands for anything in the middle. F3 repeats whichever search you used last, and Shift plus F3 goes back.
Key=F3
Hear=0 more matches for meta glasses
Note=Control plus F filters the list to what matches; Control plus Shift plus F clears it.

[step]
Say=Alt plus X is extra info: everything known about the episode the cursor is on, one field to a line, sorted by field. The cursor starts at the top.
Key=Alt+X
Hear=Extra Info: edit read only
Hear=Address https colon slash slash pinecast dot com slash listen
Note=The fields come from the page, from the play list, and from ExifTool for a file on this computer.

[step]
Say=Alt plus H is help. It leads with the keys that have no button, because those are the hardest to find.
Key=Alt+H
Hear=Player Help
Hear=PLAYER
Hear=Every control has its own Alt key, the letter underlined in its name.
Hear=These commands have no control, so they are listed first.
Hear=FINDING A TRACK
Hear=Control plus J jump to a track by name, as the list shows it
Hear=Control plus K keywords, search everything known about the tracks
Hear=F3, Shift plus F3 repeat the last jump or keyword search
Note=Read it by line, or copy it. OK closes it.

[step]
Say=Escape leaves the help. Escape again closes the Player, remembering the settings for this play list and where each episode had reached.
Key=Escape
Hear=FileDir
Hear=List box C:\PodcastDirectories
Note=Open the same page tomorrow and it starts where you stopped.

[step]
Say=That is the whole of it. One key to find a file, one key to play what is in it, Control with the arrows to move about, and Escape to come back.
Key=
Hear=
Note=Alt plus H inside the Player lists every key. F1 lists every control with what it does.
